  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Planning the Transformation**\n\nOkay, the `@google/genai` SDK is installed, version `2.4.0`. I\'m using `gemini-3.7-flash` for schema/JSON tasks and technical reasoning. The focus now is on precise planning to transform this application into the ultimate Bug Bounty and Reconnaissance orchestrator. I\'m starting by outlining the foundational system components.\n\n\n**Designing the Ingestion System**\n\nI\'m now deep in the design of the program ingestion system. The focus is on a new modal, `ProgramIngestionModal.tsx`, to handle bug bounty program links or direct policy text. This modal needs to feed an `/api/recon/parse-program` endpoint. I\'m planning to use Gemini 3.7 Flash for analysis, grounding, and structured extraction of program details like program name, platform, root domain, in-scope targets, out-of-scope conditions, safe harbor policies, and bounty structures from the policy text.\n\n\n**Defining Recon Stages**\n\nI\'m solidifying the stages in the reconnaissance flowchart. It\'s becoming clearer: Passive Recon with OSINT will kick things off, moving to Active Enumeration & DNS Resolution, then Live Probing & Port Scanning, and finally, Visual Exploration. I need to make these stages dynamically adaptable to the specific program\'s requirements, including scope guard activation, and initial workspace setup for each target upon confirmation.\n\n\n**Outlining Active Recon**\n\nI\'m now detailing the active reconnaissance phase, refining the steps. This involves WAF fingerprinting with tools like Wappalyzer and WafW00f, then diving into content discovery, JS mining, and parameter extraction with tools such as Katana, Gau, and ParamSpider. I\'m moving on to directory fuzzing and API discovery using FFuF and Kiterunner to identify potential vulnerabilities. The next stage will use vulnerability scanning with Nuclei to uncover exposures and misconfigurations. Finally, I\342\200\231ll focus on exploit chaining and report generation.\n\n\n**Implementing Live Recon Engines**\n\nI\'m making progress on the live reconnaissance engines. Each engine has an interactive status (Pending \342\236\224 Running \342\236\224 Complete). Direct action buttons will trigger actual probes via CRT.sh, DNS DoH, Wayback, and HTTP Prober right in the app. I am also integrating Red Team insights to avoid WAF bans, optimize threads, and use secret flags. Specifically, I\'m building APIs for real-time querying of CRT.sh for subdomains, DNS records via DoH, and extracting historical URLs from Wayback Machine and AlienVault OTX.\n\n\n**Developing Takeover Checks**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `/api/recon/takeover-check` API. It will capture status codes, response headers like Server, CORS, CSP, HSTS, and X-Frame-Options, technology detection, and link extraction. This API is crucial for assessing real CNAMEs against vulnerability signatures for subdomain takeover risks across various platforms like GitHub Pages, AWS S3, and more.\n\n**Constructing Command Hub**\n\nI\'m developing a One-Liners & CLI Command Hub, potentially in a `CliWorkbench.tsx` or app tab. This will serve as a central hub of professional bug bounty commands for Kali Linux or VPS. It will provide one-liners for Subdomain Discovery + Probe + Nuclei, Katana + Gau + GF Patterns for XSS, SSRF, SQLi, and redirect vulnerabilities, FFuF API Fuzzing with program headers, and Secret Finder capabilities in JavaScript files.\n\n**Defining Workspace Behavior**\n\nFinally, I\'m defining the Recon Workspace and application state. I\'m focusing on starting the app clean, without forced data, or with a clear button to \"New Program / Ingest Bug Bounty Link.\" Users can easily switch between projects or create new ones. A \"Clear Workspace\" or \"Create Real Program\" button will be available for workspace management.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 11
      nanos: 659526389
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
